WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:05.422
No, a helicopter cannot fly 90 degrees

00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:07.257
on its side like that. That's crazy.

00:00:07.257 --> 00:00:09.926
Hey, fly peeps! I am
Vernice "FlyGirl" Armour,

00:00:09.926 --> 00:00:10.760
coming at you.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:11.886
My name is Andy Stumpf.

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:15.640
I spent just under 17
years as a US Navy SEAL.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.475
My name is Adm. Jamie Foggo.

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:18.852
Peter Rhee.
Mike Elliott.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:19.853
I'm Nicholas Moran.

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.063
Today, I'll be looking
at helicopter scenes.

00:00:22.063 --> 00:00:23.189
Warship scenes.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.149
Tank-battle scenes.

00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:25.316
Battle-wound scenes.

00:00:25.316 --> 00:00:26.234
Parachuting scenes.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:28.361
Underwater action scenes from movies

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:31.031
and seeing how real they really are.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:40.623
If you need to cross the
river, cross the river.

00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:42.584
You know, there's no
reason to actually pop up

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:46.087
in the middle of it and give
away your tactical advantage.

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:47.922
If you are trying to sneak up on somebody,

00:00:47.922 --> 00:00:50.383
you would either cross the
entire thing above the water

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.844
or cross the entire thing below the water.

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:56.806
Or go farther down or
upstream and like, you know,

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:58.808
maybe find a log that's across it.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:01.436
You don't even have to get
wet 'cause being wet sucks.

00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:04.189
(dramatic music)

00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:07.942
(gunshot)

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:09.235
All right, stop right there.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:13.698
I have been asked about this
particular scene so many times

00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:16.034
because people absolutely love it

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:19.913
and they want to know how we
train to be able to do that.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.499
And here's the answer: we don't.

00:01:22.499 --> 00:01:25.043
Because right, you'd never
fall in any other direction.

00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:29.255
You just absolutely know
the exact place and time

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:30.757
that somebody's gonna fall

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:31.966
when you shoot them in the head, right?

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:32.759
Because why wouldn't you?

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:34.135
What could possibly go wrong?

00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:37.222
But you gotta remember all of
that was preceded by probably

00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:41.851
a .762 or a .300 Win Mag round
snapping through the jungle.

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:43.394
The suppressor that's on the rifle,

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:46.815
it's basically a muffler and
it contains some of the gas

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:50.860
and it drastically reduces the
decibel output of the rifle.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.281
But bullets, when they
fly through the air,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.783
they're super sonic.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:02:01.037
So it makes a crack and
there is subsonic ammunition

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:02.831
and it's much quieter.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.374
That's not what that person was shooting.

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:07.418
The whole point of doing the
charade of catching somebody

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:09.462
and lowering them into the water

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:11.798
as if it was just eating
them into the abyss,

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.216
it's 'cause you're trying to be sneaky.

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:15.969
So going through all this
in attempt to be sneaky

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:19.055
and then cracking off
a round in the jungle,

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:21.683
it defeats the entire purpose.

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:25.895
It just, they're so, it's
not how it works, people.

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:28.648
(dramatic music)

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.155
Where you link up with a submarine

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:37.448
in the middle of the ocean,
that's totally plausible.

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:38.908
They were on a zodiac,

00:02:38.908 --> 00:02:42.495
which is gonna have probably a
55 or a 35 horsepower engine.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:46.082
So they could easily go from
a land mass over the horizon

00:02:46.082 --> 00:02:49.294
out into international water
or really wherever the sub

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.880
would wanna meet and do that,
so that's totally legit.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.591
(dramatic music)

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:57.677
(door clanging shut)

00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:01.681
Yeah, wow.

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.683
So they obviously had direct support

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:06.227
from the United States
Navy to make this movie

00:03:06.227 --> 00:03:09.480
and I'm actually surprised they
showed very much of the SDV,

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.649
which stands for Seal Delivery Vehicle.

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:14.527
In essence, it is a miniature submarine.

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:18.323
It's smaller, harder to be discovered,

00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:22.285
and you can move people and
equipment a really far distance

00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:23.119
and then go back to the submarine too

00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:26.748
'cause you can go back and
relink back up to that asset

00:03:26.748 --> 00:03:27.790
that you departed from.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.585
You are in your dive gear
until that dive is over

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.213
and it can be up to 14 hours.

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.672
A lot of the times you're in dry suits.

00:03:34.672 --> 00:03:37.550
Dry and warm are two very different things

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.469
the people should not confuse.

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:40.303
They nailed it.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:42.597
It's very plausible that
all of that would occur

00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:44.015
on an SDV operation.

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:45.350
I'm gonna give 'em a 10 on that.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.144
The sniper water river catch is a zero.

00:03:48.144 --> 00:03:50.438
It actually might be a negative number.

00:03:50.438 --> 00:03:53.191
(dramatic music)

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.321
Is there a more obvious way

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:00.198
to let somebody know that you're coming

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:02.700
than white and orange
parachutes in the daytime

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.619
jumping directly over the top of them.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:05.912
Surprise!

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:07.497
You didn't see that coming.

00:04:07.497 --> 00:04:11.793
I've worn dive fins on my
feet for a free fall jump

00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:13.836
and actually was falling
through the air with them on.

00:04:13.836 --> 00:04:16.756
More often than not though,
the back strap of the fin,

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:18.341
you put your feet through that

00:04:18.341 --> 00:04:21.094
and you actually taped
the fin up to your leg.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.220
But if you're gonna land in the water,

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.138
you're gonna want to have fins on you.

00:04:24.138 --> 00:04:25.932
In case you need to
have the ability to swim

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:28.309
or exert yourself, you're
gonna need those fins.

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:33.273
(dramatic music)
(spear guns firing)

00:04:33.273 --> 00:04:34.691
(laughing)

00:04:34.691 --> 00:04:35.817
Yes.

00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:37.193
And they had a fleet,

00:04:37.193 --> 00:04:40.780
a full fleet of submersibles
with spear guns.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.785
I am not aware of any engagement
at any time in the history

00:04:46.244 --> 00:04:48.997
of human beings where anything
like this has occurred.

00:04:48.997 --> 00:04:51.541
We are not trying to get
into fights in the water,

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:52.667
like bottom line.

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:55.336
We use it as a way to
disguise what we're doing

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:58.131
or to get from A to B,
or to be sneaky, right?

00:04:58.131 --> 00:04:59.716
To like, well I don't
know where they went.

00:04:59.716 --> 00:05:01.217
Like bloop, here we
are, we're in the water.

00:05:01.217 --> 00:05:02.760
You didn't see us, surprise!

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.388
We're not looking to
get into a fight ever.

00:05:05.388 --> 00:05:08.099
So no, we're not bringing
tridents or spears

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:09.767
or any of that stuff.

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:12.520
(dramatic music)

00:05:15.106 --> 00:05:17.984
I like how they're cutting the hoses.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:21.362
Unfortunately, there is
usually an inhalation hose

00:05:21.362 --> 00:05:24.490
and an exhalation hose and they
probably could still breathe

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.700
if they caught the wrong one.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.785
We always had dive knives on us,

00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:30.246
but it's for like cutting gear
if something gets hung up.

00:05:30.246 --> 00:05:33.374
There was never a thought in my mind ever

00:05:33.374 --> 00:05:36.210
of pulling that out with the
intent of fighting with it.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.962
It was for anything but that.

00:05:37.962 --> 00:05:40.006
Like Phillip's head screws,

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:40.965
using the tip of your,

00:05:40.965 --> 00:05:43.468
like that's the only use dive knives for.

00:05:43.468 --> 00:05:46.220
(dramatic music)

00:05:48.056 --> 00:05:50.475
I like the idea of what
he's got in his mouth.

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:53.811
I'll call that the Gen One Spare Air,

00:05:53.811 --> 00:05:55.396
which with a canister of that size

00:05:55.396 --> 00:05:58.149
I feel like you have about
a one half of one breath.

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:00.777
So he certainly wouldn't
be able to stay underwater.

00:06:00.777 --> 00:06:03.529
(dramatic music)

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.286
And then the concussive
device in the water.

00:06:10.286 --> 00:06:13.748
Concussion and the way that
shock waves travel underwater

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:15.666
is a very, very real thing.

00:06:15.666 --> 00:06:18.294
Let's just say that was
a grenade of some kind.

00:06:18.294 --> 00:06:19.462
That would probably kill the people

00:06:19.462 --> 00:06:21.506
inside of that contained area,

00:06:21.506 --> 00:06:23.549
but it probably would
damage him a little bit too,

00:06:23.549 --> 00:06:25.510
especially if there's
open windows and portholes

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:27.637
'cause that stuff will bounce out as well.

00:06:27.637 --> 00:06:30.556
So it's plausible, but I would say

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.724
you would wanna get outta
the (beep) outta there

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:32.767
before that thing went off,

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:36.187
like out of the water
before that thing went off.

00:06:36.187 --> 00:06:38.564
I don't even know if I can give
this one a technical review.

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:41.859
- I found door number
three and I'm taking it.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:45.571
(dramatic music)

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.662
- Oh that's a national treasure, alright.

00:06:52.662 --> 00:06:53.788
You know jumping off of,

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:55.123
I think it was the aircraft carrier,

00:06:55.123 --> 00:06:58.209
that's probably gonna be 50, 60 feet.

00:06:58.209 --> 00:06:59.127
I'm here for that.

00:06:59.127 --> 00:07:00.294
That's definitely survivable.

00:07:00.294 --> 00:07:02.004
You could definitely do that.

00:07:02.004 --> 00:07:04.757
(dramatic music)

00:07:08.636 --> 00:07:12.056
So we navigated with, we
called them nav boards

00:07:12.056 --> 00:07:14.892
and we had three really
exciting items on there

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:17.562
that we used to get to stare
at for hours at a time.

00:07:17.562 --> 00:07:20.022
The first one you already
mentioned was a compass.

00:07:20.022 --> 00:07:23.776
The next entertaining
tool was a depth gauge,

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:26.654
and lastly a stopwatch.

00:07:26.654 --> 00:07:27.780
That was it.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.198
We dive in pairs,

00:07:29.198 --> 00:07:32.076
but only one person gets
the navigation board.

00:07:32.076 --> 00:07:35.538
So somebody else out there is
just like, "I hate my life.

00:07:35.538 --> 00:07:36.998
This dive is taking forever.

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:39.000
I don't even have anything to look at."

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.585
So sometimes when I was the buddy

00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:42.879
I would just swim on top of my dive buddy

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:44.589
and just look at the navigation board too,

00:07:44.589 --> 00:07:45.923
because I was so bored.

00:07:45.923 --> 00:07:47.592
I just wanted to look at numbers

00:07:47.592 --> 00:07:50.219
slowly just like creeping around.

00:07:50.219 --> 00:07:53.514
(dramatic music)

00:07:53.514 --> 00:07:54.932
- [Sadusky] Can you
see Gates in the water?

00:07:54.932 --> 00:07:57.685
- Sir, it's the Hudson,
nothing is visible.

00:07:57.685 --> 00:08:00.354
- So all these people that
are above them looking down

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:01.606
are gonna see the bubbles.

00:08:01.606 --> 00:08:03.399
This would be an example
of where a closed circuit

00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:04.901
would be better than open circuit.

00:08:04.901 --> 00:08:07.361
Because even though that guy
grabbed him right under water,

00:08:07.361 --> 00:08:09.614
every time they exhale,
the bubbles are coming up.

00:08:09.614 --> 00:08:12.867
It's actually really easy
to track divers' progress

00:08:12.867 --> 00:08:15.495
from above when they're
breathing open circuit.

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:16.704
Open circuit, you can see the bubbles.

00:08:16.704 --> 00:08:19.081
Closed circuit, you can be sneaky.

00:08:19.081 --> 00:08:21.042
It's just a closed loop system

00:08:21.042 --> 00:08:23.836
that only allows the gas to
flow through in one direction

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:26.130
'cause obviously you wouldn't
want it coming back and forth,

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:27.548
and you push it through
a chemical scrubber,

00:08:27.548 --> 00:08:28.925
it pulls out the carbon dioxide

00:08:28.925 --> 00:08:30.927
and boom, you're off and running.

00:08:30.927 --> 00:08:34.096
There's 100% chance that
you could actually do that.

00:08:34.096 --> 00:08:37.183
But if you wanted to do it
and actually not be seen,

00:08:37.183 --> 00:08:39.185
I'd say closed circuit is a better option.

00:08:39.185 --> 00:08:42.230
I'll give 'em a seven.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.814
- Hi, my name is Peter Rhee.

00:08:43.814 --> 00:08:46.692
I served in the military for 24 years

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.402
and I'm a trauma surgeon.

00:08:48.402 --> 00:08:50.863
I was one of the first
surgeons into Afghanistan.

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:53.658
Today, we're going to be
looking at battle wound scenes.

00:08:53.658 --> 00:08:54.492
(people yelling)

00:08:54.492 --> 00:08:55.326
- Jesus.

00:08:55.326 --> 00:08:56.160
- Bringing it in, bringing it in.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.703
- I said get his guts in now.

00:08:57.703 --> 00:08:59.705
(gunfire)

00:08:59.705 --> 00:09:01.874
- What they're trying to
show is an evisceration.

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:04.502
So a guy got hit, they're
taking enemy fire,

00:09:04.502 --> 00:09:05.920
high velocity weapons,

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:08.214
AK-47s out there getting hit

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:10.049
and then you eviscerate your organs.

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:11.676
They're making an attempt
to push it back in,

00:09:11.676 --> 00:09:12.552
you can't do it.

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:14.470
It doesn't work that way.

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:18.432
The ones I saw were from
exit wounds of AK-47s

00:09:18.432 --> 00:09:20.476
through a little small plum size hole.

00:09:20.476 --> 00:09:22.937
You will shove everything
out because you got pressure

00:09:22.937 --> 00:09:24.897
as you're grunting and
moaning from the pain

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:26.774
and then you can't shove it back in

00:09:26.774 --> 00:09:28.234
because it just pops right back out.

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:32.697
So unless they're chemically
paralyzed by intubating them,

00:09:32.697 --> 00:09:33.864
it's a waste of time.

00:09:33.864 --> 00:09:37.577
In penetrating injury when we're
talking about bullet wounds

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:38.452
and so on like that,

00:09:38.452 --> 00:09:42.039
the main thing is just get
them to me as soon as you can

00:09:42.039 --> 00:09:43.541
and stop the bleeding.

00:09:43.541 --> 00:09:45.251
If it's not your heart
and your major vessels,

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:46.085
we can save you.

00:09:46.085 --> 00:09:48.379
If you don't hit those,
the rest we can repair.

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:51.716
- Hey, hey, look at me!

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:53.843
- Now the one thing I
hate when they do movies

00:09:53.843 --> 00:09:56.178
is every time you get
some sort of torso injury,

00:09:56.178 --> 00:09:58.764
they make them spit blood
and that doesn't happen.

00:09:58.764 --> 00:10:00.641
Unless you get shot in the mouth

00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:02.226
or you have something
going through your cheeks

00:10:02.226 --> 00:10:03.644
and your throat up high.

00:10:03.644 --> 00:10:05.563
You don't vomit blood
from getting a gut shot

00:10:05.563 --> 00:10:06.397
or anything like that.

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:07.231
- Mayday, up!

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:09.025
(man groaning)
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:10:09.025 --> 00:10:09.942
- Let's move!

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:10.943
- The one thing I do like

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:12.778
is they do show how difficult it is

00:10:12.778 --> 00:10:14.071
to move a heavy body out there.

00:10:14.071 --> 00:10:18.367
These are two to 300 pound
masses that you gotta drag

00:10:18.367 --> 00:10:21.495
and two guys pulling these
things is not that easy to do.

00:10:21.495 --> 00:10:23.205
I think that part's well done.

00:10:23.205 --> 00:10:27.043
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:30.004
You'd never drop in a bird
when there's fire going on.

00:10:30.004 --> 00:10:33.799
A bird's not gonna come down
to go do a casualty evacuation

00:10:33.799 --> 00:10:35.801
when they're gonna be shooting

00:10:35.801 --> 00:10:37.720
at a $40 million bird up in the air.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.348
It'll only land when it's a
really secure landing zone.

00:10:40.348 --> 00:10:42.058
I'd give it about a six.

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:43.309
- Expected fleet.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:45.394
Priority.

00:10:46.479 --> 00:10:47.855
He's gone.

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:49.148
- It's a great movie.

00:10:49.148 --> 00:10:51.067
It's pretty well done.

00:10:51.067 --> 00:10:53.361
The triage in the field is very different

00:10:53.361 --> 00:10:55.655
than triage when you come
to a medical company.

00:10:55.655 --> 00:10:56.864
You're not doing so much of this

00:10:56.864 --> 00:10:59.700
actually during the heat
of the battle itself,

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.079
it's usually once the the
initial firefight gets done.

00:11:03.079 --> 00:11:05.039
There's four categories when we triage.

00:11:05.039 --> 00:11:07.875
So, he went through all
four of 'em dead, expectant,

00:11:07.875 --> 00:11:09.669
they need your help,
and then they're okay.

00:11:09.669 --> 00:11:11.379
The guy's hollering and yelling.

00:11:11.379 --> 00:11:14.131
If they're yelling, they're
okay, so it's a routine.

00:11:14.131 --> 00:11:17.259
And then he had one guy that's a priority,

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:18.969
and then that guy was already dead.

00:11:18.969 --> 00:11:21.013
Confirming someone that's
dead in few seconds

00:11:21.013 --> 00:11:22.181
is not easy to do.

00:11:22.181 --> 00:11:26.102
I was in Ramadi when we had
200 people that were bombed

00:11:26.102 --> 00:11:27.645
at one time from one bomb.

00:11:27.645 --> 00:11:30.439
We got 70 of these casualties
that came right to us

00:11:30.439 --> 00:11:33.192
just coming in and they
all come in one by one

00:11:33.192 --> 00:11:34.694
and I was the triage officer.

00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:36.987
The first marine I got was expecting,

00:11:36.987 --> 00:11:39.115
but I couldn't tell for sure

00:11:39.115 --> 00:11:42.743
because I only had literally
four or five seconds

00:11:42.743 --> 00:11:44.620
to decide whether he was dead or not,

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.498
and there was no way we
could use our resources

00:11:47.498 --> 00:11:48.332
to work on them.

00:11:48.332 --> 00:11:52.211
So what you're doing is trying
to cherry pick the easy saves

00:11:52.211 --> 00:11:54.130
so you don't want to to
spend too much time on it,

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:57.341
which is very different than
what we do in civilian trauma.

00:11:57.341 --> 00:12:01.554
In civilian trauma we go full
bore, hardcore, team of 12

00:12:01.554 --> 00:12:03.681
on top of one person for hours and hours.

00:12:03.681 --> 00:12:05.975
And that's not something
you can do in the military.

00:12:05.975 --> 00:12:07.601
I give it at nine.

00:12:07.601 --> 00:12:08.644
- Stay with me, stay with me.

00:12:08.644 --> 00:12:10.563
- Talk to me, guys, talk
to me, what happened?

00:12:10.563 --> 00:12:11.731
- Is that Kirk?

00:12:11.731 --> 00:12:13.274
(beep) man.

00:12:13.274 --> 00:12:14.692
- There's some realism in there.

00:12:14.692 --> 00:12:16.193
This is an outpost.

00:12:16.193 --> 00:12:17.611
This looks like Army.

00:12:17.611 --> 00:12:19.363
So army Italian base station.

00:12:19.363 --> 00:12:20.698
Army will usually have a medic

00:12:20.698 --> 00:12:22.658
or they'll have a physician's assistant.

00:12:22.658 --> 00:12:24.994
Usually with as much webbing and stuff,

00:12:24.994 --> 00:12:26.746
the unit that they're in, the housing unit

00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:28.122
is paper thin.

00:12:28.122 --> 00:12:29.790
So the bullets will go
right through there.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.876
They should be a little bit more fortified

00:12:31.876 --> 00:12:34.837
with the Hesco barriers or dirt.

00:12:34.837 --> 00:12:36.672
You notice that the guy is bagging a guy

00:12:36.672 --> 00:12:39.300
'cause he's not breathing well
and they're about to do CPR.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:40.468
We don't do that out there.

00:12:40.468 --> 00:12:42.428
If they need CPR, that's wasting time

00:12:42.428 --> 00:12:43.554
because they need blood.

00:12:43.554 --> 00:12:46.056
- Medevac won't be here for a few hours,

00:12:46.056 --> 00:12:48.642
which means he needs fresh
blood if he wants to live.

00:12:48.642 --> 00:12:49.477
This is the only thing we can do.

00:12:49.477 --> 00:12:51.228
We're not sterile and we
can't test for disease.

00:12:51.228 --> 00:12:53.189
Our fresh blood is all
we can do to save him.

00:12:53.189 --> 00:12:54.940
- Gloves again with the bloody hands,

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:56.442
that's not really realistic.

00:12:56.442 --> 00:12:57.777
They have gloves at this point.

00:12:57.777 --> 00:12:58.611
When I went out there,

00:12:58.611 --> 00:12:59.862
we started doing this for the first time,

00:12:59.862 --> 00:13:03.491
we actually started to really
use warm, fresh whole blood,

00:13:03.491 --> 00:13:05.284
which is miraculous.

00:13:05.284 --> 00:13:06.827
It's unbelievably miraculous.

00:13:06.827 --> 00:13:08.829
We can't do that in the civilian world.

00:13:08.829 --> 00:13:10.539
But we did walking blood bank.

00:13:10.539 --> 00:13:15.085
In my unit in Ramadi, Charlie
Med, two surgeon shop,

00:13:15.085 --> 00:13:18.964
we had about 60-70 medics with us as well.

00:13:18.964 --> 00:13:21.550
The base was about 600 people.

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:23.385
So we'd go to the chow hall

00:13:23.385 --> 00:13:25.012
and there would be a guy that screams out

00:13:25.012 --> 00:13:26.555
"Walking blood bank."

00:13:26.555 --> 00:13:28.682
Everybody would spit out their food,

00:13:28.682 --> 00:13:32.561
go line up at my medical
company, check their dog tags,

00:13:32.561 --> 00:13:35.564
they'd lay down on a
floor, they would poke 'em,

00:13:35.564 --> 00:13:37.024
get a bag of blood.

00:13:37.024 --> 00:13:38.192
We would do a little test

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:40.152
to see if they were
compatible, and we used it.

00:13:40.152 --> 00:13:42.446
So from that perspective, it's true.

00:13:42.446 --> 00:13:45.115
There's a lot of little mistakes
that they made through here

00:13:45.115 --> 00:13:46.534
in order to dramatize it

00:13:46.534 --> 00:13:48.786
and they can get away
with it for most people,

00:13:48.786 --> 00:13:51.247
but you know, you wouldn't
be walking up to 'em

00:13:51.247 --> 00:13:54.333
and giving them your blood
standing up and things like that.

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:55.459
At least they're wearing
the right uniform,

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:56.669
so I give 'em seven.

00:13:56.669 --> 00:14:01.674
(explosion)
(man screaming)

00:14:01.966 --> 00:14:03.300
This is a really good scene.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.594
When that explosion
went off in slow motion,

00:14:05.594 --> 00:14:07.721
you could see the boot
flying right at the camera.

00:14:07.721 --> 00:14:10.516
It's a little bit unusual
from an injury point of view.

00:14:10.516 --> 00:14:12.518
A shell coming in hitting the ground

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:14.562
would take his legs off like that

00:14:14.562 --> 00:14:17.857
because seeing bilateral
amputees at the thigh

00:14:17.857 --> 00:14:20.943
has been a really recent
injury that we never saw before

00:14:20.943 --> 00:14:21.944
in previous wars.

00:14:21.944 --> 00:14:25.447
We didn't really see bilateral
amputations above the knee.

00:14:25.447 --> 00:14:27.324
Before, in the other war movies,

00:14:27.324 --> 00:14:30.369
they would step on claymores and landmines

00:14:30.369 --> 00:14:32.663
and those were actually meant to take off

00:14:32.663 --> 00:14:34.206
your foot and your ankle.

00:14:34.206 --> 00:14:35.958
So the medics would come out

00:14:35.958 --> 00:14:38.711
and then you'd sniper the
medics and get more casualties.

00:14:38.711 --> 00:14:41.338
So it wasn't really supposed
to be a mortal wound.

00:14:41.338 --> 00:14:42.965
His was mangled below the knee,

00:14:42.965 --> 00:14:45.134
but obviously his was going to be close

00:14:45.134 --> 00:14:46.969
to what we were seeing in Iraq

00:14:46.969 --> 00:14:51.098
where they would just
build these huge bombs

00:14:51.098 --> 00:14:52.725
and bury them into the ground.

00:14:52.725 --> 00:14:56.395
We were seeing just legs blown sky high.

00:14:56.395 --> 00:14:59.648
(man grunting)

00:14:59.648 --> 00:15:01.609
I could do three hours on tourniquets.

00:15:01.609 --> 00:15:04.904
Blood loss is the number
one preventable loss

00:15:04.904 --> 00:15:05.821
that we want to avoid.

00:15:05.821 --> 00:15:07.114
Some of the soldiers,

00:15:07.114 --> 00:15:09.491
they even wear tourniquets
when they're going out there.

00:15:09.491 --> 00:15:12.202
Actually what we are
recommending and what we teach

00:15:12.202 --> 00:15:14.163
is that they put on your own tourniquets.

00:15:14.163 --> 00:15:16.999
The tourniquets are problematic

00:15:16.999 --> 00:15:19.585
because if you don't put it on right

00:15:19.585 --> 00:15:23.130
and if it's not on absolutely
the tightest it can be,

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.090
you'll bleed more and you'll bleed out.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:27.092
When you put it on,
you gotta commit to it.

00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:28.510
You gotta go full bore.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.554
And I tell you, putting a tourniquet on

00:15:30.554 --> 00:15:32.556
will be the most painful
thing you experience

00:15:32.556 --> 00:15:33.766
about three to four minutes.

00:15:33.766 --> 00:15:35.351
It's like your leg is falling off.

00:15:35.351 --> 00:15:38.437
So, it is something you
do as a last resort.

00:15:38.437 --> 00:15:41.440
It will save lives, but
you gotta go for it,

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.400
otherwise you will actually bleed more.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:44.985
(man groaning)

00:15:44.985 --> 00:15:46.695
- Here comes the morphine,
here comes the morphine.

00:15:46.695 --> 00:15:48.072
It's gonna kick in real quick, Ralph.

00:15:48.072 --> 00:15:49.531
We're gonna get you shifted.

00:15:49.531 --> 00:15:51.742
- In the old days, morphine was good.

00:15:51.742 --> 00:15:53.327
We got scared of it

00:15:53.327 --> 00:15:55.245
because morphine can definitely kill you.

00:15:55.245 --> 00:15:57.915
If you take a person
who's low blood pressure,

00:15:57.915 --> 00:16:00.084
not a blood circulating
and then you give it,

00:16:00.084 --> 00:16:02.836
then it can depress your cardiac function

00:16:02.836 --> 00:16:05.005
and then you can die, you
would also stop breathing.

00:16:05.005 --> 00:16:07.466
So we've actually gone away
from morphine in the field.

00:16:07.466 --> 00:16:09.176
We use a new drug called ketamine.

00:16:09.176 --> 00:16:11.971
While it provides a lot of relief of pain,

00:16:11.971 --> 00:16:13.681
it does not lower your blood pressure.

00:16:13.681 --> 00:16:15.140
So, that's the reason why we use it.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:18.644
The medics can do a lot and
they're so vitally important,

00:16:18.644 --> 00:16:20.562
but a lot of the movies is,

00:16:20.562 --> 00:16:23.816
I think the audience doesn't
understand that a medic

00:16:23.816 --> 00:16:25.025
is not a trauma surgeon.

00:16:25.025 --> 00:16:27.528
Trauma surgeon obviously
went to medical school,

00:16:27.528 --> 00:16:29.571
did five years of general surgery,

00:16:29.571 --> 00:16:31.365
then two years of training
as a trauma surgeon.

00:16:31.365 --> 00:16:33.492
So seven years of training
like a brain surgeon.

00:16:33.492 --> 00:16:35.661
The medic's job is to get 'em to us,

00:16:35.661 --> 00:16:37.538
not to clamp things in the field.

00:16:37.538 --> 00:16:38.580
It's pretty good.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.332
I'd say give it a nine.

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:42.084
- My name is Admiral Jamie Foggo

00:16:42.084 --> 00:16:44.712
and I served in the Navy
for almost 40 years.

00:16:44.712 --> 00:16:46.171
When I retired I took over

00:16:46.171 --> 00:16:49.049
as Dean of the Center
for Maritime Strategy

00:16:49.049 --> 00:16:51.427
of the Navy League here in Washington, DC.

00:16:51.427 --> 00:16:55.305
Today, we're gonna be looking
at warship scenes from movies.

00:16:55.305 --> 00:16:58.767
(dramatic music)

00:16:58.767 --> 00:17:00.519
- Second torpedo to starboard beam!

00:17:00.519 --> 00:17:01.645
Bearing 1-2-0.

00:17:01.645 --> 00:17:04.398
(dramatic music)

00:17:05.357 --> 00:17:08.944
- "Greyhound" is an
absolutely phenomenal movie.

00:17:08.944 --> 00:17:13.949
It tells and depicts with
great historical accuracy.

00:17:13.949 --> 00:17:17.036
The Germans are depicted in the movie

00:17:17.036 --> 00:17:19.872
as deploying the wolf pack
tactics, and you know,

00:17:19.872 --> 00:17:22.666
you see two torpedoes coming
from different directions,

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:24.918
you know that's more than
one submarine firing.

00:17:24.918 --> 00:17:27.755
And these tactics were
extremely effective.

00:17:27.755 --> 00:17:29.757
(dramatic music)

00:17:29.757 --> 00:17:30.883
- Meter, meter!

00:17:30.883 --> 00:17:33.218
(dramatic music)

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:34.887
- So what you see on
the bridge of the ship

00:17:34.887 --> 00:17:36.722
is an attack on the destroyer.

00:17:36.722 --> 00:17:39.391
I think that was very realistic.

00:17:39.391 --> 00:17:42.186
And you see the wake of the
torpedo coming towards you.

00:17:42.186 --> 00:17:45.105
The propellers do something
that we call cavitate.

00:17:45.105 --> 00:17:48.025
They create bubbles as they're
going through the water

00:17:48.025 --> 00:17:50.736
and that creates a trail
behind the torpedo.

00:17:50.736 --> 00:17:54.156
And so, a sharp lookout
can pick that up early

00:17:54.156 --> 00:17:57.659
and it gives the commanding
officer an advantage

00:17:57.659 --> 00:18:01.038
to conduct those drastic
evasive maneuvers.

00:18:01.038 --> 00:18:03.415
If it was nighttime, a lot of times

00:18:03.415 --> 00:18:05.250
it's even easier to see the torpedo

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.544
because of the
phosphorescence in the water.

00:18:07.544 --> 00:18:09.588
It kind of glows as it's coming at you.

00:18:09.588 --> 00:18:11.715
A lot of these torpedoes
were electric powered.

00:18:11.715 --> 00:18:14.343
They had batteries and
electric motors in them

00:18:14.343 --> 00:18:16.136
and they would go towards the target.

00:18:16.136 --> 00:18:19.598
Nowadays we have torpedoes
that are propelled by fuel

00:18:19.598 --> 00:18:21.141
and have a very long range.

00:18:21.141 --> 00:18:23.268
And so it was a different game back then,

00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:26.105
but in this case, absolutely accurate.

00:18:26.105 --> 00:18:28.857
(dramatic music)

00:18:33.487 --> 00:18:35.864
As far as the maneuverability is concerned

00:18:35.864 --> 00:18:37.199
of that destroyer,

00:18:37.199 --> 00:18:39.868
you have to keep in mind
this is not a battleship

00:18:39.868 --> 00:18:42.287
that displaces 50,000 tons.

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:45.249
These are lighter ships nowadays,

00:18:45.249 --> 00:18:47.668
heavy destroyer in the United States Navy

00:18:47.668 --> 00:18:49.920
is about 7,800-8,000 tons.

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.840
The propulsion systems
on these destroyers,

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:55.092
the engines and the
propellers and the rudders

00:18:55.092 --> 00:18:58.303
are made for speed and maneuverability.

00:18:58.303 --> 00:19:01.807
Particularly because in the
Second Battle of the Atlantic,

00:19:01.807 --> 00:19:03.642
they were not only going
after surface ships,

00:19:03.642 --> 00:19:04.852
they were going after submarines.

00:19:04.852 --> 00:19:07.479
And that means you've gotta
be able to turn on a dime.

00:19:07.479 --> 00:19:08.897
(dramatic music)

00:19:08.897 --> 00:19:11.900
(ships guns firing)

00:19:13.152 --> 00:19:15.946
The rounds that actually hit the water

00:19:15.946 --> 00:19:17.698
or penetrate the water,

00:19:17.698 --> 00:19:20.534
whether or not they actually
do any damage, it depends.

00:19:20.534 --> 00:19:24.538
As soon as you know a .50
caliber round hits water,

00:19:24.538 --> 00:19:26.206
typically it ricochets.

00:19:26.206 --> 00:19:28.625
If it does go beneath the water line,

00:19:28.625 --> 00:19:31.170
you lose velocity very quickly.

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:32.838
So, it's highly unlikely

00:19:32.838 --> 00:19:35.382
that that would penetrate
any kind of steel hull.

00:19:35.382 --> 00:19:37.217
But again, you never know.

00:19:37.217 --> 00:19:42.222
(dramatic music)
(ships guns firing)

00:19:42.639 --> 00:19:45.809
So he has to surface
because water's coming in.

00:19:45.809 --> 00:19:47.477
And then, while he's on the surface,

00:19:47.477 --> 00:19:50.939
they finish him off in pretty
much of a brutal manner.

00:19:50.939 --> 00:19:54.026
But you see what looks
like anti-aircraft guns

00:19:54.026 --> 00:19:56.320
that are lowered to the deck plate level.

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.864
As this projectile went in and it blew up,

00:19:58.864 --> 00:20:01.366
it may have caused secondary explosions.

00:20:01.366 --> 00:20:04.453
First there's an initial
boom and then a massive boom.

00:20:04.453 --> 00:20:08.624
There's so much oil and fuel and weapons

00:20:08.624 --> 00:20:11.084
on board that submarine that, you know,

00:20:11.084 --> 00:20:14.213
once it took that initial
penetration of the hull

00:20:14.213 --> 00:20:16.715
and those shape charges
went in and exploded.

00:20:16.715 --> 00:20:19.927
This movie gets absolutely
top marks for me.

00:20:19.927 --> 00:20:22.930
As far as realism, 10 outta 10.

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.640
- Cradle lowered.

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:26.099
- Right, I'll ram the projectile.

00:20:26.099 --> 00:20:28.685
- Easy!

00:20:28.685 --> 00:20:30.562
- When you ram that
powder, you ram it easy.

00:20:30.562 --> 00:20:32.272
- Real easy.

00:20:32.272 --> 00:20:34.191
- This is one of the most accurate movies

00:20:34.191 --> 00:20:38.737
on how you load a 16-inch
projectile into a 16-inch barrel.

00:20:38.737 --> 00:20:41.740
That projectile weighs 2,000 pounds.

00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:45.494
So you're essentially
loading a a Volkswagen beetle

00:20:45.494 --> 00:20:48.121
into the breach of that gun
and firing it at the enemy.

00:20:48.121 --> 00:20:50.415
And you notice that the projectile,

00:20:50.415 --> 00:20:53.001
it's followed by these powder charges.

00:20:53.001 --> 00:20:56.046
This was a very accurate representation

00:20:56.046 --> 00:20:57.297
of how you load that gun.

00:20:57.297 --> 00:20:59.341
There are spark proof tools

00:20:59.341 --> 00:21:01.301
and everybody is very careful

00:21:01.301 --> 00:21:02.886
because you know that powder

00:21:02.886 --> 00:21:05.389
is obviously explosive and volatile

00:21:05.389 --> 00:21:09.101
and any mistake or any spark
or any flame could set it off

00:21:09.101 --> 00:21:10.686
and blow up that turret.

00:21:10.686 --> 00:21:12.479
- 2-0-4 degrees, Calloway.

00:21:12.479 --> 00:21:14.606
(beeping)

00:21:14.606 --> 00:21:15.524
2-0-4 degrees.

00:21:15.524 --> 00:21:16.775
- 2-0-4 degrees.

00:21:16.775 --> 00:21:21.530
- This very shiny brass
device, that's a gun site,

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:25.784
and that's to allow the gun
captain to take a look out

00:21:25.784 --> 00:21:28.328
and see the direction that he's firing in.

00:21:28.328 --> 00:21:30.914
That's the way that we used to do it.

00:21:30.914 --> 00:21:34.793
Before we had these very
sophisticated fire control radars

00:21:34.793 --> 00:21:37.963
and gun directors that are
on top of the turrets today.

00:21:37.963 --> 00:21:39.214
- My fire in the hole.

00:21:41.091 --> 00:21:43.719
(explosion)
(man yelling)

00:21:43.719 --> 00:21:45.595
- I got a chuckle out of the fact

00:21:45.595 --> 00:21:49.516
that Tommy Lee Jones runs
toward the 16-inch gun turret

00:21:49.516 --> 00:21:50.642
just before it's fired.

00:21:50.642 --> 00:21:52.811
We had, on the battleships, procedures

00:21:52.811 --> 00:21:55.772
to prevent sailors from being topside

00:21:55.772 --> 00:21:58.483
in proximity to those turrets
when they fire the guns,

00:21:58.483 --> 00:22:01.111
because the over pressure of the blast

00:22:01.111 --> 00:22:04.948
blew 'em back, you know,
a dozen or so feet.

00:22:04.948 --> 00:22:07.367
And that's probably fairly accurate.

00:22:07.367 --> 00:22:09.786
Even today, with our missile systems,

00:22:09.786 --> 00:22:13.040
if you're launching from the
fore deck of a destroyer,

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:14.583
nobody's up there when you're launching

00:22:14.583 --> 00:22:17.836
because there's gonna be
a lot of flash to bang

00:22:17.836 --> 00:22:19.212
as the weapon comes out the tube

00:22:19.212 --> 00:22:20.797
and you don't want anybody to get hurt.

00:22:20.797 --> 00:22:23.467
I'd give it a seven outta 10.

00:22:23.467 --> 00:22:26.219
(dramatic music)

00:22:28.889 --> 00:22:32.726
"Battleship" happens to be
one of my favorite movies.

00:22:32.726 --> 00:22:35.604
These are World War II era ships.

00:22:35.604 --> 00:22:37.689
They're not part of the
active fleet anymore,

00:22:37.689 --> 00:22:40.692
but these ships are made to fight

00:22:40.692 --> 00:22:42.611
and when you're on one
of those battleships

00:22:42.611 --> 00:22:46.365
and you have a belt of armor around you,

00:22:46.365 --> 00:22:51.370
12 to 17 inches thick
and 50,000 tons of steel

00:22:51.995 --> 00:22:55.707
to protect you, that's pretty reassuring.

00:22:55.707 --> 00:22:56.750
- Drop port anchor.

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:57.793
- What?

00:22:57.793 --> 00:22:58.627
(anchor clanging)

00:22:58.627 --> 00:23:01.463
(water splashing)

00:23:06.301 --> 00:23:08.553
- The officer of the deck on the bridge

00:23:08.553 --> 00:23:13.392
uses the anchor on the
battleship to avoid getting hit

00:23:13.392 --> 00:23:16.311
by some of the aliens weapons.

00:23:16.311 --> 00:23:18.397
Frankly, that's pretty hokey.

00:23:18.397 --> 00:23:21.191
The amount of chain on a
battleship or an aircraft carrier

00:23:21.191 --> 00:23:25.070
connected to the anchor is pretty long.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:27.155
You know, it's probably
at least 1,000 feet

00:23:27.155 --> 00:23:29.449
but we don't use anchors for that reason.

00:23:29.449 --> 00:23:32.244
We use anchors to more the ship in port

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:36.581
and this is in the Pacific
Ocean, which is really deep.

00:23:36.581 --> 00:23:39.084
(guns firing)

00:23:39.084 --> 00:23:43.338
(dramatic music)
(guns firing)

00:23:43.338 --> 00:23:46.341
One thing that the
viewers might not notice

00:23:46.341 --> 00:23:48.552
is it's so powerful

00:23:48.552 --> 00:23:52.556
that the ship actually moves
sideways several yards.

00:23:52.556 --> 00:23:54.015
When you fire a broad side,

00:23:54.015 --> 00:23:55.600
oh it can move significantly

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:57.894
and you have to do a
course correction there.

00:23:57.894 --> 00:23:59.646
I'd probably give it a two.

00:23:59.646 --> 00:24:01.857
I think Hollywood took
a little poetic license

00:24:01.857 --> 00:24:03.358
on the anchor in this case.

00:24:03.358 --> 00:24:06.194
- Hey, fly peeps, I am
Vernice Fly Girl Armor

00:24:06.194 --> 00:24:07.696
coming at you.

00:24:07.696 --> 00:24:08.613
And back in 2003,

00:24:08.613 --> 00:24:12.117
I became America's first
Black female combat pilot

00:24:12.117 --> 00:24:14.035
serving two tours in Iraq.

00:24:14.035 --> 00:24:16.788
Today, I'll be looking at
helicopter scenes in movies.

00:24:16.788 --> 00:24:19.541
(dramatic music)

00:24:21.001 --> 00:24:23.420
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:25.964
Flying in formation in
helicopters is very common.

00:24:25.964 --> 00:24:27.215
We practice it a lot.

00:24:27.215 --> 00:24:29.593
We will land together,
we'll take off together.

00:24:29.593 --> 00:24:30.969
And in an environment like this

00:24:30.969 --> 00:24:32.804
where you have that mission
where you're dropping off

00:24:32.804 --> 00:24:36.349
say a flight of soldiers in this case,

00:24:36.349 --> 00:24:39.561
yeah that would absolutely
happen like that.

00:24:39.561 --> 00:24:43.899
(helicopter blades whirring)
(dramatic music)

00:24:43.899 --> 00:24:46.485
(explosion)

00:24:46.485 --> 00:24:49.237
Just everything about
that combat maneuver,

00:24:49.237 --> 00:24:51.072
the insertion, them dropping.

00:24:51.072 --> 00:24:54.701
That's why we don't do static
things like that anymore

00:24:54.701 --> 00:24:58.121
in combat because of the vulnerability

00:24:58.121 --> 00:25:02.626
and the helicopter has pretty
much no maneuverability

00:25:02.626 --> 00:25:03.835
when you're in that close

00:25:03.835 --> 00:25:05.128
to be able to get out really quick,

00:25:05.128 --> 00:25:06.838
a helicopter just doesn't move quick.

00:25:06.838 --> 00:25:11.384
A helicopter has to build up
momentum and get going, right?

00:25:11.384 --> 00:25:15.305
RPGs are a huge threat to helicopters,

00:25:15.305 --> 00:25:18.225
especially if we're flying
what we call low and slow.

00:25:18.225 --> 00:25:19.893
But I remember flying in
combat and we just saw the,

00:25:19.893 --> 00:25:21.478
and it was early on in combat

00:25:21.478 --> 00:25:23.355
and I just saw these great clouds of mist

00:25:23.355 --> 00:25:24.189
and we were trying to figure out

00:25:24.189 --> 00:25:25.857
like what are those clouds of mist?

00:25:25.857 --> 00:25:29.945
And they were RPGs that had
been programmed to explode

00:25:29.945 --> 00:25:31.821
at 200 and 300 feet.

00:25:31.821 --> 00:25:35.116
And some of my buddies
were impacted by RPGs.

00:25:35.116 --> 00:25:36.159
Huge threat.

00:25:36.159 --> 00:25:39.079
(helicopter blades whirring)
(people yelling)

00:25:39.079 --> 00:25:41.331
(crashing)

00:25:42.666 --> 00:25:45.627
So the one I would say
unrealistic part of that clip.

00:25:45.627 --> 00:25:49.589
When that RPG hit the tail
rotor of that aircraft,

00:25:49.589 --> 00:25:52.926
I thought it would've
been spinning a lot faster

00:25:52.926 --> 00:25:56.346
and it wasn't gliding down without power.

00:25:56.346 --> 00:25:58.056
And at the very end,

00:25:58.056 --> 00:26:00.684
if he tried to cushion
the landing a little bit,

00:26:00.684 --> 00:26:02.936
which we're taught to do
in that kind of situation

00:26:02.936 --> 00:26:04.312
with the loss of tail rotor,

00:26:04.312 --> 00:26:06.314
we would've tried to circle around

00:26:06.314 --> 00:26:08.024
in the direction that it's spinning

00:26:08.024 --> 00:26:09.776
to have a little control.

00:26:09.776 --> 00:26:12.153
But it wouldn't have just landed flat

00:26:12.153 --> 00:26:13.780
like you saw in that video.

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:15.156
So the guys in the back,

00:26:15.156 --> 00:26:16.324
hopefully they're tied in

00:26:16.324 --> 00:26:18.076
or they would just
immediately be thrown out

00:26:18.076 --> 00:26:19.786
just like a car accident that twirls over

00:26:19.786 --> 00:26:21.955
and everybody gets ejected
out of the vehicle.

00:26:21.955 --> 00:26:26.710
The reason I think they
show or film more Helos

00:26:26.710 --> 00:26:27.836
getting hit in the tail rotor

00:26:27.836 --> 00:26:29.045
just 'cause it's more dramatic.

00:26:29.045 --> 00:26:30.171
You get hit in the tail rotor,

00:26:30.171 --> 00:26:31.840
you're immediately gonna start spinning

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.049
and go outta control.

00:26:33.049 --> 00:26:34.509
If you get hit in the main rotor,

00:26:34.509 --> 00:26:36.094
you just explode and fall out the sky.

00:26:36.094 --> 00:26:37.596
Which one's more exciting?

00:26:37.596 --> 00:26:40.015
On a scale of one to 10, nine.

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:42.350
(dramatic music)

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:44.853
(guns firing)

00:26:46.104 --> 00:26:48.106
Guns at the front of
the attack helicopters.

00:26:48.106 --> 00:26:48.898
Is that realistic?

00:26:48.898 --> 00:26:49.733
Absolutely.

00:26:49.733 --> 00:26:52.485
I had a 20 millimeter
gatlin gun, three barrel,

00:26:52.485 --> 00:26:56.781
it rolled around and
delivered lots of bullets

00:26:56.781 --> 00:26:58.366
very fast on target.

00:26:58.366 --> 00:27:00.118
So yes, 100% realistic.

00:27:00.118 --> 00:27:02.120
Only the pilots would fire that weapon.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.831
(dramatic music)

00:27:04.831 --> 00:27:06.541
You see two rocket launchers left,

00:27:06.541 --> 00:27:08.877
right then two missile
launchers left and right.

00:27:08.877 --> 00:27:12.088
There are 26 rockets in
each one of those pods.

00:27:12.088 --> 00:27:15.425
Then there are four
missiles on the end board.

00:27:15.425 --> 00:27:17.719
That is actually a standard payload

00:27:17.719 --> 00:27:19.179
for an attack helicopter.

00:27:19.179 --> 00:27:21.514
(rockets firing)

00:27:21.514 --> 00:27:24.184
(explosions)

00:27:24.184 --> 00:27:27.937
The rockets are called 2.75-inch rockets.

00:27:27.937 --> 00:27:30.982
So they're about this big
around, right, 2.75 inches

00:27:30.982 --> 00:27:33.276
and they're pretty long.

00:27:33.276 --> 00:27:36.404
The rockets are just like bullets.

00:27:36.404 --> 00:27:38.031
You aim, you shoot, you pull,

00:27:38.031 --> 00:27:41.076
they go wherever wind shear impacts them.

00:27:41.076 --> 00:27:45.372
The hellfire missiles
are laser guided, right?

00:27:45.372 --> 00:27:48.500
So they're guided by a laser
to a particular target.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.585
A rocket, it impacts a small area.

00:27:50.585 --> 00:27:52.837
Let's say it's 20 feet, 30 feet.

00:27:52.837 --> 00:27:55.423
These rockets are
basically just, you know,

00:27:55.423 --> 00:27:58.635
as soon as they hit, they
explode and it's a point target.

00:27:58.635 --> 00:28:03.640
The missile has a lot more
explosive power to it.

00:28:03.973 --> 00:28:06.643
You could shoot a tank or a
building, it's gonna explode.

00:28:06.643 --> 00:28:08.978
There are heat seeking missiles

00:28:08.978 --> 00:28:13.525
and missiles that can fly to
catch a jet per se, right?

00:28:13.525 --> 00:28:16.069
But those aren't the kind of
missiles that, number one,

00:28:16.069 --> 00:28:17.195
were on this aircraft.

00:28:17.195 --> 00:28:21.700
And number two, helicopters
don't normally carry them.

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:26.579
And they don't follow humans.

00:28:26.579 --> 00:28:29.457
And I doubt his jet pack had enough heat

00:28:29.457 --> 00:28:32.168
coming off 'cause it probably
would've burned up his legs

00:28:32.168 --> 00:28:33.628
if that were the case.

00:28:33.628 --> 00:28:36.381
(dramatic music)

00:28:38.216 --> 00:28:41.803
There would've been no way
they could just glide into

00:28:41.803 --> 00:28:44.264
going through the vortices
of the helicopter.

00:28:44.264 --> 00:28:48.226
I could easily see them being
blown down 50 to 100 feet

00:28:48.226 --> 00:28:49.436
just like that.

00:28:49.436 --> 00:28:51.855
The payload, that was actually realistic.

00:28:51.855 --> 00:28:53.815
The heat seeking missiles, not so much.

00:28:53.815 --> 00:28:55.483
I'm gonna give it a seven.

00:28:55.483 --> 00:28:57.736
(explosion)

00:28:57.736 --> 00:28:58.903
(helicopter blades whirring)
(dramatic music)

00:28:58.903 --> 00:29:01.322
You can't fly through something like that.

00:29:01.322 --> 00:29:02.240
Unrealistic.

00:29:03.199 --> 00:29:05.744
Rotors would've already
fallen off the aircraft.

00:29:05.744 --> 00:29:07.871
As hard as rotor blades look,

00:29:07.871 --> 00:29:10.915
they're very sensitive and
finely tuned and balanced.

00:29:10.915 --> 00:29:13.460
And as soon as it impacts an object,

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:15.962
you're not gonna be able to
fly the aircraft after that.

00:29:15.962 --> 00:29:16.796
- Hurry up!

00:29:17.964 --> 00:29:20.633
- Because it's The Rock,
could have been realistic

00:29:20.633 --> 00:29:21.843
with him hanging on the side.

00:29:21.843 --> 00:29:23.595
It's like hanging onto a car, right?

00:29:23.595 --> 00:29:26.806
Because most of the
downward push of the wind

00:29:26.806 --> 00:29:30.852
from a helicopter is on the
last third of the blades.

00:29:30.852 --> 00:29:32.645
It's not in the in the very center.

00:29:32.645 --> 00:29:35.857
So, the wind shear
wouldn't have pushed him

00:29:35.857 --> 00:29:36.983
out of the middle.

00:29:39.486 --> 00:29:40.695
Totally unrealistic.

00:29:40.695 --> 00:29:41.905
It's just like the wheel of a car.

00:29:41.905 --> 00:29:43.072
It's never in one position.

00:29:43.072 --> 00:29:44.532
It's constantly moving.

00:29:44.532 --> 00:29:46.910
Where'd he get the piece
of metal to even fix it?

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.954
When they showed it in that
clip, it was just stable.

00:29:49.954 --> 00:29:52.040
He was actually able
to hold it and the rod.

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:54.709
It's just not gonna be stable like that.

00:29:54.709 --> 00:29:58.046
(person yelling)

00:29:58.046 --> 00:30:00.340
No, a helicopter can not fly 90 degrees

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:01.341
on its side like that.

00:30:01.341 --> 00:30:02.175
That was crazy.

00:30:02.175 --> 00:30:04.719
I would give it a one.

00:30:04.719 --> 00:30:05.553
- Greetings all.

00:30:05.553 --> 00:30:06.387
I'm Nicholas Moran.

00:30:06.387 --> 00:30:08.014
I'm commissioned as an Armor Officer

00:30:08.014 --> 00:30:10.600
on M1 tanks and then M3 Bradleys.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:13.102
These days, my work as
a civilian historian

00:30:13.102 --> 00:30:14.896
for the publisher of World of Tanks.

00:30:14.896 --> 00:30:17.440
Today we're gonna be looking
at tank battle scenes.

00:30:18.525 --> 00:30:20.860
(explosion)

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:24.489
And that is absolutely accurate.

00:30:24.489 --> 00:30:26.866
The vast majority of
tanks that were destroyed

00:30:26.866 --> 00:30:29.244
had no idea that the enemy was there.

00:30:29.244 --> 00:30:31.246
And again, this is why
you always have to work

00:30:31.246 --> 00:30:32.539
in conjunction with infantry

00:30:32.539 --> 00:30:34.541
because they have more eyes than you do.

00:30:34.541 --> 00:30:36.125
They have better vision than you do.

00:30:36.125 --> 00:30:37.126
But more often than not,

00:30:37.126 --> 00:30:40.004
the first indication
that somebody is there

00:30:40.004 --> 00:30:41.381
is when you get shot at.

00:30:41.381 --> 00:30:43.758
And knocking off either
the first or the last tank

00:30:43.758 --> 00:30:45.385
in an ambush in a row
like that is classic.

00:30:45.385 --> 00:30:49.973
(people yelling)
(dramatic music)

00:30:49.973 --> 00:30:51.891
- 88, it's a goddamn tank.

00:30:51.891 --> 00:30:53.893
- With regard to the TCs in this case,

00:30:53.893 --> 00:30:57.480
keeping their heads out, there
are two schools of thought,

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:59.399
but certainly in the time
period of World War II

00:30:59.399 --> 00:31:01.693
all the way through the
Cold War, even myself,

00:31:01.693 --> 00:31:03.862
it would be in a head out position.

00:31:03.862 --> 00:31:08.032
The Western school of thought
is you keep your head out,

00:31:08.032 --> 00:31:10.785
because what you don't see will kill you.

00:31:10.785 --> 00:31:11.911
The other school of thought,

00:31:11.911 --> 00:31:15.039
such as used by the Soviet
Army in the Cold War

00:31:15.039 --> 00:31:17.333
was that you wanted to operate head down.

00:31:17.333 --> 00:31:18.418
So you close the hatch.

00:31:18.418 --> 00:31:21.713
No artillery could affect you,
no snipers could affect you.

00:31:21.713 --> 00:31:24.883
And the problem of course is the reverse.

00:31:24.883 --> 00:31:27.719
You aren't able to see
whatever is about to kill you.

00:31:27.719 --> 00:31:31.097
So there is a balance to be struck.

00:31:31.097 --> 00:31:33.600
- Right, all tanks move forward.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:37.145
(tanks accelerating)

00:31:37.145 --> 00:31:38.813
- War daddy has decided for some reason

00:31:38.813 --> 00:31:41.274
that he's going to charge the enemy tank,

00:31:41.274 --> 00:31:43.651
I'm not sure I would've
done quite the same thing

00:31:43.651 --> 00:31:47.113
because that simplifies
the problem for the enemy

00:31:47.113 --> 00:31:49.240
and just give them
three targets in a line.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:52.577
If you can have what's
called a base of fire

00:31:52.577 --> 00:31:54.287
and then a maneuver element,

00:31:54.287 --> 00:31:56.039
then the enemy tank has a choice.

00:31:56.039 --> 00:31:59.626
He can engage the guy
that's just sitting there

00:31:59.626 --> 00:32:01.544
pumping accurate shots at you,

00:32:01.544 --> 00:32:04.589
or the German tank can
engage the flanking element

00:32:04.589 --> 00:32:06.174
who are coming around the side

00:32:06.174 --> 00:32:08.217
and the 76-millimeter
gun on two of these tanks

00:32:08.217 --> 00:32:09.969
actually would destroy a Tiger.

00:32:09.969 --> 00:32:12.805
(muffled yelling)

00:32:14.641 --> 00:32:16.392
Okay, so being obscured,

00:32:16.392 --> 00:32:19.395
the German tank has decided
to advance out of the smoke

00:32:19.395 --> 00:32:20.647
as opposed to just sit there

00:32:20.647 --> 00:32:23.232
and wait for whatever the
enemy were going to do.

00:32:23.232 --> 00:32:25.985
The Germans were very, very
big on individual initiative.

00:32:25.985 --> 00:32:29.238
The concept is called Auftragstaktik.

00:32:29.238 --> 00:32:31.032
These days we use a development of it

00:32:31.032 --> 00:32:32.617
called Mission Command.

00:32:32.617 --> 00:32:35.954
But the bottom line is
you don't wait for orders,

00:32:35.954 --> 00:32:39.248
you take the initiative, you
make a decision on the spot.

00:32:39.248 --> 00:32:41.751
And that is exactly what
the German has done here.

00:32:43.252 --> 00:32:45.338
- Fivel, put it up his (beep)
where the armor's thin.

00:32:45.338 --> 00:32:46.756
- I know to (beep) hit him.

00:32:46.756 --> 00:32:50.176
- The radical is accurate for
a World War II American tank.

00:32:50.176 --> 00:32:53.012
And Shia, in this case is
using the coaxial telescope.

00:32:53.012 --> 00:32:55.473
So you're gonna see
graduations on the radical.

00:32:55.473 --> 00:32:59.310
Each graduation is for a type
of round at a certain range.

00:32:59.310 --> 00:33:02.730
So if your target is let's say 800 yards,

00:33:02.730 --> 00:33:06.818
you will elevate the gun so
that the 800 yard graduation

00:33:06.818 --> 00:33:07.944
is on the target.

00:33:07.944 --> 00:33:10.989
So the single biggest cause
of missing in World War II

00:33:10.989 --> 00:33:12.907
was range estimation error.

00:33:12.907 --> 00:33:14.158
If you get the range wrong,

00:33:14.158 --> 00:33:16.494
it doesn't matter if your aim is correct,

00:33:16.494 --> 00:33:18.246
you will go over or short.

00:33:18.246 --> 00:33:20.331
Which is why we now have
modern fire control systems

00:33:20.331 --> 00:33:23.209
to do all that mathematics for you.

00:33:23.209 --> 00:33:25.670
(bullets clanging)

00:33:25.670 --> 00:33:26.504
- Again!

00:33:26.504 --> 00:33:29.257
(dramatic music)

00:33:30.133 --> 00:33:32.301
- If you're in this sort of a knife fight,

00:33:32.301 --> 00:33:33.970
which I wouldn't recommend getting into.

00:33:33.970 --> 00:33:35.930
The first round set the engine alight,

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.266
but that doesn't necessarily
say that the tank crew

00:33:38.266 --> 00:33:40.685
and turret can't still get a shot off.

00:33:40.685 --> 00:33:43.229
You say you shoot at a tank until it burns

00:33:43.229 --> 00:33:44.689
or changes shape.

00:33:44.689 --> 00:33:47.275
So keep shooting until you
know that the threat is gone.

00:33:47.275 --> 00:33:49.569
This scene is an easy nine.

00:33:49.569 --> 00:33:53.322
What "Fury" lacks in perhaps
some of the technical accuracy,

00:33:53.322 --> 00:33:55.700
it more than makes up for an understanding

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:58.161
the experience of being a tank crewman.

00:33:58.161 --> 00:34:00.288
(dramatic music)

00:34:00.288 --> 00:34:03.583
(gun squeaking)

00:34:03.583 --> 00:34:04.709
This is absolutely correct.

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:07.503
There's gun crews on
each side of the Sponson.

00:34:07.503 --> 00:34:11.049
The tank is based off of
a Mark VII International,

00:34:11.049 --> 00:34:13.801
which was a very late World War I tank

00:34:13.801 --> 00:34:16.846
built by both the UK and US.

00:34:16.846 --> 00:34:19.057
And at the time, British tanks,

00:34:19.057 --> 00:34:21.225
the heavy tanks had what
were called Sponson guns.

00:34:21.225 --> 00:34:23.811
The turret, that wasn't on the Mark VIII.

00:34:23.811 --> 00:34:25.980
(yelling)

00:34:27.815 --> 00:34:30.735
Shouting instructions to
each other wouldn't happen.

00:34:30.735 --> 00:34:31.819
It's too loud.

00:34:31.819 --> 00:34:33.279
Inside a World War I tank,

00:34:33.279 --> 00:34:36.157
the entire middle of that tank
was taken up by the engine.

00:34:36.157 --> 00:34:37.492
It was massive noise.

00:34:37.492 --> 00:34:39.285
What he's doing there,
he's using hand signals.

00:34:39.285 --> 00:34:40.745
That's the only way to communicate

00:34:40.745 --> 00:34:42.288
inside a World War I tank.

00:34:42.288 --> 00:34:44.916
(dramatic music)

00:34:44.916 --> 00:34:46.876
(explosion)

00:34:46.876 --> 00:34:50.838
Firing a gun with anything
in the barrel is bad.

00:34:50.838 --> 00:34:52.799
You're under risk of
exactly what happened here.

00:34:52.799 --> 00:34:54.217
It's called petaling.

00:34:54.217 --> 00:34:58.471
When the gun barrel just
petals out like a flower

00:34:58.471 --> 00:34:59.597
and your gun is done.

00:34:59.597 --> 00:35:03.518
Oftentimes you'll see a tank
will have a rubber muzzle cap

00:35:03.518 --> 00:35:06.604
on the end purely to stop any dirt at all

00:35:06.604 --> 00:35:08.106
from getting into it.

00:35:08.106 --> 00:35:09.732
And it's designed that you can actually

00:35:09.732 --> 00:35:10.817
shoot through the muzzle cap.

00:35:10.817 --> 00:35:13.569
(dramatic music)

00:35:16.114 --> 00:35:18.616
To my knowledge, on tank fighting

00:35:18.616 --> 00:35:20.827
has never been trained in any army.

00:35:20.827 --> 00:35:22.912
Personally, I'd have just
stuck my head out the top,

00:35:22.912 --> 00:35:24.163
used a pistol, but again,

00:35:24.163 --> 00:35:26.666
not very dramatic and it
will kind of suck for Indie.

00:35:26.666 --> 00:35:28.000
I would have to give the scene

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:31.337
maybe a three on feasibility grounds.

00:35:31.337 --> 00:35:36.342
(dramatic music)
(people yelling)

00:35:36.425 --> 00:35:38.261
A Tiger has appeared on the scene.

00:35:38.261 --> 00:35:41.222
A Tiger had a mythical
status in World War II.

00:35:41.222 --> 00:35:44.892
It had a reputation because
it was very, very hard to kill

00:35:44.892 --> 00:35:46.519
and because it had a heck of a gun,

00:35:46.519 --> 00:35:49.689
the 8.8 centimeter was lethal
to basically everything.

00:35:49.689 --> 00:35:51.983
The problem was that it looks
very much like a Panzer IV,

00:35:51.983 --> 00:35:53.025
which is a lot more common.

00:35:53.025 --> 00:35:55.653
In actuality, historians
have found very few times

00:35:55.653 --> 00:35:57.488
on the western front
that American soldiers

00:35:57.488 --> 00:35:59.157
ever actually met a Tiger.

00:35:59.157 --> 00:36:01.534
But for the purposes of
drama, a Tiger is fine.

00:36:01.534 --> 00:36:04.287
(dramatic music)

00:36:05.204 --> 00:36:06.914
This was pretty realistic.

00:36:06.914 --> 00:36:10.042
Drivers are absolutely
blind inside their tanks

00:36:10.042 --> 00:36:11.711
and they're utterly
reliant on instructions

00:36:11.711 --> 00:36:13.045
from the commander.

00:36:13.045 --> 00:36:15.298
So that's how you would
get up close to a tank

00:36:15.298 --> 00:36:17.175
low below the line of vision,

00:36:17.175 --> 00:36:19.302
below the the level of depression

00:36:19.302 --> 00:36:20.678
that the guns can aim at you.

00:36:20.678 --> 00:36:23.431
(tank squeaking)

00:36:24.849 --> 00:36:26.142
They're going for the wheels.

00:36:26.142 --> 00:36:27.935
Because the Tiger was a breakthrough tank,

00:36:27.935 --> 00:36:30.146
it was heavily armored everywhere.

00:36:30.146 --> 00:36:32.231
And if you're infantry,

00:36:32.231 --> 00:36:34.066
and you don't have high-velocity cannons,

00:36:34.066 --> 00:36:36.569
your best bet is to disable the tank

00:36:36.569 --> 00:36:38.154
and get what's called a mobility kill.

00:36:38.154 --> 00:36:39.405
To immobilize the tank.

00:36:39.405 --> 00:36:41.616
Your two choices are gonna be
either the engine compartment

00:36:41.616 --> 00:36:43.534
or the running gear.

00:36:43.534 --> 00:36:46.454
And the only thing that these sticky bombs

00:36:46.454 --> 00:36:47.830
are going to have any particular effect on

00:36:47.830 --> 00:36:48.706
will be the running gear.

00:36:48.706 --> 00:36:51.459
So the sticky bomb, it was in the manual,

00:36:51.459 --> 00:36:53.920
but it is also almost a
weapon of desperation.

00:36:53.920 --> 00:36:56.589
The military is supposed to
give you tools for the job.

00:36:56.589 --> 00:36:58.883
Late World War II era, you're
talking about the bazooka.

00:36:58.883 --> 00:37:00.760
Now the problem is that you
only have so many rounds

00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:03.638
of bazooka ammunition and a
bazooka is only so effective

00:37:03.638 --> 00:37:06.015
against something as
heavily armored as a Tiger.

00:37:06.015 --> 00:37:09.101
So you're in desperation
mode to start with.

00:37:09.101 --> 00:37:10.436
- Both sides!

00:37:10.436 --> 00:37:11.562
Get aboard!

00:37:11.562 --> 00:37:12.396
Get a grenade!

00:37:12.396 --> 00:37:13.773
- So now we see one of the problems

00:37:13.773 --> 00:37:15.650
with a tank going on its own.

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:18.402
A tank shouldn't go on its
own for several reasons,

00:37:18.402 --> 00:37:20.696
but when you have infantry,

00:37:20.696 --> 00:37:23.532
an infestation we might
like to say as tankers,

00:37:23.532 --> 00:37:25.117
crawling on your tank, there's a process

00:37:25.117 --> 00:37:27.245
that's called scratching
your friend's back.

00:37:27.245 --> 00:37:28.496
What you do is you get on the radio,

00:37:28.496 --> 00:37:30.248
you call your buddy with a machine gun

00:37:30.248 --> 00:37:32.416
and he will machine gun your tank.

00:37:32.416 --> 00:37:33.960
Of course the bullets aren't
gonna affect your tank,

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:34.794
you're in a tank,

00:37:34.794 --> 00:37:37.713
but it will very much
clean off, or delouse,

00:37:37.713 --> 00:37:39.757
the infantry that are
crawling on your tank.

00:37:39.757 --> 00:37:43.052
(guns firing)

00:37:43.052 --> 00:37:44.595
And that's your example of delousing.

00:37:44.595 --> 00:37:46.847
So, the 20-millimeter anti-aircraft gun,

00:37:46.847 --> 00:37:49.058
like any anti-aircraft gun
with a high rate of fire,

00:37:49.058 --> 00:37:50.768
you aim that thing horizontally

00:37:50.768 --> 00:37:52.687
and you will do a number on infantry.

00:37:52.687 --> 00:37:55.231
If there's any big myth about tanks,

00:37:55.231 --> 00:37:58.484
it's probably that they're
these invulnerable fortresses.

00:37:58.484 --> 00:38:00.278
In reality, they're
not invulnerable at all

00:38:00.278 --> 00:38:03.197
and they're absolutely
reliant on the other parts

00:38:03.197 --> 00:38:04.448
of the Combined Arms team.

00:38:04.448 --> 00:38:07.660
You have the desperation
of lightly armed infantry.

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:09.245
You have this back scratching.

00:38:09.245 --> 00:38:12.832
You have the immobilization
of a very dangerous tank.

00:38:12.832 --> 00:38:15.167
I mean this is easily on the nine scale.

00:38:15.167 --> 00:38:18.587
- Hi, I'm Mike Elliott,
a 26-year combat veteran.

00:38:18.587 --> 00:38:19.505
I served 11 years

00:38:19.505 --> 00:38:21.674
with the US Army Golden
Knight Parachute team.

00:38:21.674 --> 00:38:25.553
I have over 16,000
skydives, 6,000 tandems,

00:38:25.553 --> 00:38:29.181
100 static line jumps and about
20 military free fall jumps.

00:38:29.181 --> 00:38:30.182
Today we're gonna be looking

00:38:30.182 --> 00:38:32.059
at parachuting scenes in movies.

00:38:32.059 --> 00:38:34.520
(dramatic music)

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:36.272
- Come on, come on, come on.

00:38:36.272 --> 00:38:37.565
- So I think you did
the correct procedures.

00:38:37.565 --> 00:38:40.484
With military free fall
jumpers with combat equipment,

00:38:40.484 --> 00:38:42.528
they have their O2 masks on, goggles.

00:38:42.528 --> 00:38:43.696
It's a lot of moving parts.

00:38:43.696 --> 00:38:46.991
So there is a possibility that
something could come loose.

00:38:46.991 --> 00:38:50.911
It takes about 15 seconds
to to fix his O2 pipe

00:38:50.911 --> 00:38:52.038
and put it back to its helmet.

00:38:52.038 --> 00:38:54.957
You can hold your breath up
to a minute and still be okay.

00:38:54.957 --> 00:38:57.084
So he had plenty of time to fix his mask

00:38:57.084 --> 00:38:58.210
and still leave the aircraft.

00:38:58.210 --> 00:39:01.130
(dramatic music)

00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:03.090
His body position is very correct here.

00:39:03.090 --> 00:39:05.217
He's in what we call a dive position.

00:39:05.217 --> 00:39:07.386
And it is so uber important

00:39:07.386 --> 00:39:09.638
to have the correct body position.

00:39:09.638 --> 00:39:12.808
Basically you have to learn
how to push air with your body.

00:39:12.808 --> 00:39:15.061
So the wind is basically
hitting him around his waist

00:39:15.061 --> 00:39:15.895
and chest area,

00:39:15.895 --> 00:39:19.523
which allows that wind to be
symmetrical around his body

00:39:19.523 --> 00:39:22.443
so he can gain stability
after he exits the aircraft.

00:39:22.443 --> 00:39:24.278
- 25,000 feet.

00:39:24.278 --> 00:39:27.698
- You did a very great
job as a HALO jumper.

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:31.577
Basically, HALO is high
altitude, low opening,

00:39:31.577 --> 00:39:34.288
meaning you exit the
aircraft above 15,000 feet.

00:39:34.288 --> 00:39:37.375
You have to have some type
of breathing apparatus

00:39:37.375 --> 00:39:39.585
in the aircraft as well as on your body.

00:39:39.585 --> 00:39:41.045
As far as the form is concerned,

00:39:41.045 --> 00:39:42.880
there's no different form with HALO.

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:46.092
It's called the the box man
position or the arch position.

00:39:46.092 --> 00:39:48.719
Everything he's doing in
this scene is done correctly.

00:39:48.719 --> 00:39:51.389
Your knees are bent, your
pelvis is pushed forward,

00:39:51.389 --> 00:39:53.099
your arms are back.

00:39:53.099 --> 00:39:55.684
(dramatic music)

00:39:55.684 --> 00:39:57.436
That is a pretty gnarly looking storm.

00:39:57.436 --> 00:40:01.399
I have never had to jump
into clouds and lightning,

00:40:01.399 --> 00:40:02.608
and it's not even approved

00:40:02.608 --> 00:40:04.276
by the Federal Aviation Administration.

00:40:04.276 --> 00:40:06.278
With clouds, you cannot see the ground

00:40:06.278 --> 00:40:08.572
and you can get struck by lightning.

00:40:08.572 --> 00:40:10.032
And if you flying with other jumpers,

00:40:10.032 --> 00:40:12.701
now you at a risk of
running into another person.

00:40:12.701 --> 00:40:16.038
And when you slam into another
person at 120 miles per hour,

00:40:16.038 --> 00:40:17.748
it's gonna be some serious injuries.

00:40:17.748 --> 00:40:20.334
(heavy breathing)

00:40:20.334 --> 00:40:21.710
- 4,000.

00:40:21.710 --> 00:40:23.671
Deploy, deploy, deploy!

00:40:23.671 --> 00:40:27.550
- I do like that the body
position of both actors

00:40:27.550 --> 00:40:28.717
in this movie are correct.

00:40:28.717 --> 00:40:29.802
And if you're unconscious

00:40:29.802 --> 00:40:31.345
and you're going to be on your back

00:40:31.345 --> 00:40:32.972
because your arms and legs

00:40:32.972 --> 00:40:34.265
are the lowest portions of your body.

00:40:34.265 --> 00:40:36.767
So gravity will put you on your back.

00:40:36.767 --> 00:40:40.062
I would rate this a high seven for sure.

00:40:40.062 --> 00:40:42.398
All those are very unique maneuvers

00:40:42.398 --> 00:40:43.732
that could possibly happen.

00:40:48.988 --> 00:40:51.407
(air rushing)

00:40:51.407 --> 00:40:55.411
A total high five on this scene
from the ramp to the exit.

00:40:55.411 --> 00:40:57.663
With a military free fall operation,

00:40:57.663 --> 00:41:01.542
each group that's exiting
aircraft has a team leader

00:41:01.542 --> 00:41:02.710
and you're guiding all the guys.

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:05.045
So they're grouping around him

00:41:05.045 --> 00:41:06.464
so they can see what's going on.

00:41:06.464 --> 00:41:09.425
And that's the same formation they stay in

00:41:09.425 --> 00:41:12.052
during their exit and as
they get out into free fall.

00:41:14.054 --> 00:41:15.556
As you see here, they
jumping at nighttime.

00:41:15.556 --> 00:41:18.058
Everything gets really,
really difficult at nighttime

00:41:18.058 --> 00:41:20.603
because you're either flying through NODs

00:41:20.603 --> 00:41:22.146
or just with your naked eye

00:41:22.146 --> 00:41:23.481
and you can't see obstacles,

00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:24.815
you can't see the ground coming up.

00:41:24.815 --> 00:41:27.943
And in the army you have
the round static line jumps

00:41:27.943 --> 00:41:29.778
from like the 82nd Air 1 Division

00:41:29.778 --> 00:41:31.155
and you have the army parachute team,

00:41:31.155 --> 00:41:34.241
which are demonstrations
and competitive type guys.

00:41:34.241 --> 00:41:35.826
On the Special Operation side,

00:41:35.826 --> 00:41:39.914
US Navy Seals or Delta
Forces or Special Forces,

00:41:39.914 --> 00:41:41.749
these guys are trained
to put that parachute

00:41:41.749 --> 00:41:44.335
where they need to put it to
do what they need to do best.

00:41:44.335 --> 00:41:48.005
So if these guys are
illustrating from a spec op side,

00:41:48.005 --> 00:41:50.466
yes, that is something that
can definitely be done.

00:41:50.466 --> 00:41:53.219
(dramatic music)

00:41:54.094 --> 00:41:55.596
In this particular scene,

00:41:55.596 --> 00:41:58.224
they're doing a HALO,
special operation soldiers,

00:41:58.224 --> 00:42:00.893
their thing is to be as
stealthy and as quiet

00:42:00.893 --> 00:42:02.311
and as dark as they can.

00:42:02.311 --> 00:42:04.355
And the premise of a HALO jump

00:42:04.355 --> 00:42:08.150
is soldiers could step out
up to 40 or 50 miles away

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:09.610
at 25,000 feet,

00:42:09.610 --> 00:42:11.862
open their parachute by 20,000 feet

00:42:11.862 --> 00:42:15.074
and they can drift in quietly, stealthily,

00:42:15.074 --> 00:42:17.451
undetected from miles away.

00:42:17.451 --> 00:42:19.787
For this, I don't know
why they would have smoke,

00:42:19.787 --> 00:42:21.705
especially, all you can't
see smoke at nighttime.

00:42:21.705 --> 00:42:25.125
It is mandatory to have
blinking lights on your body,

00:42:25.125 --> 00:42:26.544
when you're flying at nighttime,

00:42:26.544 --> 00:42:29.171
so other aircraft could see you.

00:42:29.171 --> 00:42:30.381
Special Operation guys,

00:42:30.381 --> 00:42:32.925
they're not gonna have
any lights and flares

00:42:32.925 --> 00:42:35.886
because they're coming in as stealthily,

00:42:35.886 --> 00:42:36.971
as quiet as possible

00:42:36.971 --> 00:42:39.723
so they can do the mission
once they get on the ground.

00:42:43.185 --> 00:42:46.272
The landing, as you see,
it's a extremely tight area.

00:42:46.272 --> 00:42:49.066
The tight parachutes that
military special op guys,

00:42:49.066 --> 00:42:51.610
are called MC-4/MC-5s.

00:42:51.610 --> 00:42:54.446
Very big, very docile parachutes.

00:42:54.446 --> 00:42:58.284
They definitely can land on
buildings and tight areas.

00:42:58.284 --> 00:43:00.536
I don't think they would
land in something that tight.

00:43:00.536 --> 00:43:01.829
Not a bad looking clip at all.

00:43:01.829 --> 00:43:03.289
I would give them a six.

00:43:03.289 --> 00:43:06.625
(people yelling)

00:43:06.625 --> 00:43:08.794
(dramatic music)

00:43:08.794 --> 00:43:12.881
Being able to grip and hold
on is virtually impossible.

00:43:12.881 --> 00:43:15.009
That parachute is designed for one person

00:43:15.009 --> 00:43:16.385
and you're not hooked up properly

00:43:16.385 --> 00:43:17.636
as if you're doing a tandem.

00:43:17.636 --> 00:43:19.221
But if I knew there was a possibility

00:43:19.221 --> 00:43:21.056
that parachute could save both our lives,

00:43:21.056 --> 00:43:23.976
then I would make that attempt to do that.

00:43:23.976 --> 00:43:26.729
(dramatic music)

00:43:27.896 --> 00:43:29.857
I was trying to look and see

00:43:29.857 --> 00:43:32.234
what their alterminal reading was.

00:43:32.234 --> 00:43:35.154
I couldn't really see, I see
it's flashing low altitude,

00:43:35.154 --> 00:43:37.698
but we have no idea of how low they are

00:43:37.698 --> 00:43:39.491
before they open up their main parachute.

00:43:39.491 --> 00:43:42.578
2,500 feet is when you open
up your main parachute.

00:43:42.578 --> 00:43:45.998
Takes time for those things to fully open.

00:43:45.998 --> 00:43:48.375
With today's parachute systems,

00:43:48.375 --> 00:43:51.045
we have what we call a
automatic opening device.

00:43:51.045 --> 00:43:53.505
It's gonna open up the
reserve parachute for you

00:43:53.505 --> 00:43:56.383
and that is designed to
open at a low altitude.

00:43:56.383 --> 00:43:57.801
So your main parachute,

00:43:57.801 --> 00:43:59.553
which they're using in this scene,

00:43:59.553 --> 00:44:02.765
they could open up at
that lower of an altitude

00:44:02.765 --> 00:44:04.141
is not very realistic.

00:44:04.141 --> 00:44:08.270
(dramatic music)
(person screaming)

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:11.774
To have someone attached
to you and land on a target

00:44:11.774 --> 00:44:14.151
that's not doing a tandem,
absolutely impossible.

00:44:14.151 --> 00:44:15.944
It's called parachute accuracy.

00:44:15.944 --> 00:44:17.363
Two people under one parachute,

00:44:17.363 --> 00:44:19.448
somebody's gonna break
some ankles for sure.

00:44:19.448 --> 00:44:21.367
In that case, they should
have did what we call

00:44:21.367 --> 00:44:22.743
a parachute landing fall,

00:44:22.743 --> 00:44:25.537
where you hit the balls of
your feet, calve, thighs,

00:44:25.537 --> 00:44:27.873
and your buttocks, which is
the softest part of your body.

00:44:27.873 --> 00:44:29.917
So trying to land and roll

00:44:29.917 --> 00:44:31.418
to those soft sides of your body

00:44:31.418 --> 00:44:33.337
is kind of what they did in this scene,

00:44:33.337 --> 00:44:35.673
but there's just no way
that you can even see

00:44:35.673 --> 00:44:38.550
around that person enough
to fly that parachute

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:39.760
to a target area.

00:44:39.760 --> 00:44:42.137
I'm gonna rate these guys at a five.

00:44:45.516 --> 00:44:49.687
Wow, I mean, every time I
see a static line operation

00:44:49.687 --> 00:44:51.814
at this depth, it gives me chill bumps

00:44:51.814 --> 00:44:54.400
to think about soldiers in World War II,

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:55.984
the forefathers of parachuting.

00:44:55.984 --> 00:44:57.277
With static line jumping,

00:44:57.277 --> 00:44:58.862
it's the most expedient way

00:44:58.862 --> 00:45:00.406
to put soldiers on a battlefield.

00:45:00.406 --> 00:45:02.533
If you look at some of the actual footage

00:45:02.533 --> 00:45:05.077
that happened on D-Day and Market Garden,

00:45:05.077 --> 00:45:06.120
that's basically what you see.

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:10.124
Numerous, numerous parachutes
in the air in any given time.

00:45:10.124 --> 00:45:12.876
(lines creaking)

00:45:13.961 --> 00:45:16.213
So, this is absolutely 100% correct.

00:45:16.213 --> 00:45:19.174
I was actually an airborne
instructor for three years

00:45:19.174 --> 00:45:21.969
where I trained parachuters
how to become paratroopers.

00:45:21.969 --> 00:45:24.888
And the best way to get out
the aircraft is basically

00:45:24.888 --> 00:45:26.515
you pushing the guy in
front of you out the door

00:45:26.515 --> 00:45:29.226
so you can get out the
door as quick as possible,

00:45:29.226 --> 00:45:32.146
and as you exit, you get into
a good tight body position,

00:45:32.146 --> 00:45:33.856
which is your hands over your harness,

00:45:33.856 --> 00:45:34.857
feet and knees together.

00:45:34.857 --> 00:45:37.901
And that static line actually
pulls your parachute out

00:45:37.901 --> 00:45:40.195
and your parachute opens automatically.

00:45:40.195 --> 00:45:41.822
I'm gonna rate this as a 10.

00:45:41.822 --> 00:45:44.533
Just amazing any and every time I see

00:45:44.533 --> 00:45:47.035
what the guy's done for
us back in World War II.

